  "decision_conditions": [
    "The culmulative impact of the existing and proposed development at the site,&nbsp;comprised of various ad-hoc additions and alterations to the original dwelling and its roofscape, will dominate the dwelling&nbsp;and no&nbsp;longer bear resemblance to the&nbsp;character of the original&nbsp;dwellinghouse,&nbsp;resulting in harm to the appearance of the dwelling, built form and symmetry of the semi-detached pair,&nbsp;and character of the wider area,&nbsp;notably given the location of the application site on a prominent corner plot and siting&nbsp;opposite the&nbsp;Grade I listed Eastbury Manor House,&nbsp; and&nbsp;within the Becontree Estate. As such, the proposed development is contrary to: - National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF)&nbsp;(DLUHC ,December 2023); - Policy D4 of the London Plan (March 2021); -Policies CP2 and CP3 of the Local Development Framework (LDF) Core Strategy (July 2010); - Policies BP2 and BP11 of the Local Development Framework (LDF) Borough Wide Development Plan Document (DPD) (March 2011); - Policies SP2, DMD1, DMD4 and DMD6 of The London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission Version, December 2021)",
    "&nbsp;The scale, siting and design of the proposed development would result in direct overlooking an increased sense of overbearingness to the rear garden at no. 36 Denham Way, which is intensified by the close proximity between the development and no.36, therefore constituting unneighbourly development and contrary to: - National Planning Policy Framework National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, December 2023); - Policy D4 of the London Plan (March 2021);; - Policy CP3 of Local Development Framework (LDF) Core Strategy (July 2010); - Policies BP8 and BP11 of the Local Development Framework (LDF) Borough Wide Development Plan Document (DPD) (March 2011); - The Residential Extensions and Alterations Supplementary Planning Document (February 2012); - Policies SP2, DMD1 and DMD6 of The London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission Version, December 2021)",
    "The application has failed to demonstrate that the proposed development would accommodate appropriate off-street car parking provision, noting that the site does not appear to benefit from any existing lawful crossovers. As such, it cannot be ensured that the development will not negatively impact the existing residents and users of the surrounding area. The application is therefore contrary to: - National Planning Policy Framework National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, December 2023); - Policy T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021); - Policies BR9 and BR10 of the Local Development Framework (LDF) Borough Wide Development Plan Document (DPD) (March 2011); - Policy DMT3 of The London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission Version, December 2021)",
    "The proposed dwelling (no.4a Eastbury Square)&nbsp; provides very poor outlook at ground floor and will suffer from a sense of enclosure. In addition to both proposed gardens being undersized, the garden provided at no.4a does not appear to be useable given its shape, which has not been accurately portrayed in the block plan provided. As such, the proposed development introduces sub-standard accommodation that will not facilitate an acceptable living standard for future occupiers, and represents a reduced standard of residential accommodation than the existing family sized dwelling. The proposed development is therefore contrary to: - National Planning Policy Framework (DLUHC ,December 2023); - Policies D4 and D6 of the London Plan (March 2021); - Policies CP3 and CC1 of the LDF Core Strategy (July 2010); - Policies BP6, BP8 and BP11 of the LDF Borough Wide Development Plan Policies DPD (March 2011); - Policies SP2, SP3 and DMD1 of the Draft Local Plan (Regulation 19 Submission version, December 2021); -DCLG Technical Housing Standards (nationally described space standard) (DCLG, March 2015) (as amended)."
  ],
